00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:13.860
With the invention of the telescope in the 17th century, humans were finally able to

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:18.480
see a magnified view of the infinite space above our planet.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:26.260
In that space, we saw the colossal planets and moons of our solar system, the dazzling

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:29.720
palettes of nebulae and distant galaxies and

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.200
the darkness of the unknown, but most importantly, we saw potential.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:48.040
As our first and only true home away from home, the International Space Station has become

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:52.370
the essential hub for multi-discipline scientific research in space.

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:58.340
To further our understanding of the effects of long-duration spaceflight on the human

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:58.780
body.

00:14:59.410 --> 00:14:59.970
Astronomers

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.620
Scott Kelly and cosmonaut Mikhail Korniyanko spent a continuous year aboard the station.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:13.820
The duo returned in March with data that will allow NASA to learn more about the effects

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:17.080
of microgravity and radiation on the human body.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.640
The ISS is continuously occupied by an alternating international team of pilots, commanders and

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:29.980
specialists whose work on station this year included the sequencing of more than

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:37.030
1 billion base pairs of DNA, advanced fire suppression experimentation, and several hardware

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:44.370
upgrades. One such upgrade was the International Docking Adapter, which enables future crews to

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:53.460
arrive via Boeing's CST-100 Starlighter and SpaceX's Crew Dragon spacecraft. As American

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:59.840
commercial crew spacecraft continue development and grow ever closer to readiness, Soyuz remains

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.170
the singular method for expedition crew chain. In addition to six crewed Soyuz

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:11.180
arrivals and departures, several uncrewed agency and commercial flights delivered

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:15.950
and returned cargo, supplies and experiments to the station throughout the

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:23.220
year. The Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency or JAXA successfully delivered

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:29.790
six tons of cargo to the ISS with the H-2 transfer vehicle. The SpaceX Dragon

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.640
capsule delivered the Bigelow expandable activity module BEAM to ISS in April, which

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:42.720
was subsequently installed and inflated. BEAM is currently undergoing air quality

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:50.360
checks, testing, and monitoring. In a hallmark return to flight, Orbital ATK once again launched

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:57.680
the Cygnus capsule atop the Antares rocket, carrying 5,100 pounds of supplies and experiments

00:16:57.680 --> 00:16:58.560
to the ISS.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:07.990
SpaceX's Dragon cargo capsule visited the station three more times, also delivering necessary supplies and experiments.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:17.500
Additionally, the Falcon 9 first stage achieved its first ever landing on the drone ship, P.S. I Still Love You, in April,

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:21.040
followed by five additional successful landings.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:29.980
One of those additional landings achieved another milestone when the first stage was reused to launch a communications set.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.040
in March. Blue Origin's New Shepard capsule aced its first in-flight escape

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:42.360
test, verifying earlier ground tests of the full envelope escape system. The same

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:47.290
test saw the fifth autonomous vertical landing of the New Shepard first stage

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:54.180
booster. Virgin Galactic Spaceship Two returned to free flight in December as

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:57.300
preparation for further powered flights later this year.

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:09.700
The Orion Program continued development of the next-generation human exploration spacecraft,

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:15.750
surpassing multiple splashdown, parachute, and Pacific Ocean recovery tests.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:21.300
Orion's completed heat shield arrived at Kennedy Space Center in Florida,

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:27.770
ready for integration with the capsule for 2018's Exploration Mission 1.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:29.900
Core State

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.580
fuel tank welding was completed for NASA's new heavy lift vehicle, the Space

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:40.590
Launch System. Additionally, the SLS program managers completed successful

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:46.170
testing of the solid rocket boosters and RS-25 engines that will power its

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:51.960
flight. To prepare SLS and Orion for flight, the Kennedy Space Center

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:56.680
underwent a comprehensive modernization review of upgraded launch pad systems

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.040
and new platforms in the VAB.

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:07.830
The Curiosity rover found chemicals in Martian rocks that suggest it once had more oxygen in its atmosphere.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:12.800
Curiosity also conducted an in-place study of active sand dunes,

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:16.170
chose its own rock targets for laser spectrometry,

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:21.460
and sent back stunning photos of the Martian landscape and the oddities within.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:29.910
The New Horizons mission sent the last bits of science data from Pluto and will now run

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.740
with an object deeper within the Kuiper Belt.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:38.890
The stunning images and important scientific data sent to us by New Horizons

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:44.350
has fundamentally changed the way we view the Ice Dwarf and its newly discovered moons.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:50.400
In September, NASA launched the OSIRIS-REx sampling spacecraft

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:56.150
that will rendezvous with the asteroid Bennu and return a sample in 2023.

00:19:57.710 --> 00:19:59.750
NASA also approved the asteroid

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:09.550
redirect missions next phase of robotic design and development launched in 2011 the juno spacecraft

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:17.520
arrived at jupiter on the fourth of july to study the origins of the gas giant juno will measure

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:24.110
jupiter's atmosphere map its magnetic and gravity fields and study the magnetosphere near jupiter's

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:29.780
poles. And finally, looking deep into space, the Kepler mission has verified

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.980
thousands of additional new exoplanets, many of which are considered Earth-like.

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:43.140
Tonight, as robotic explorers roam the surface of a neighboring planet and probe

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:47.940
the outer reaches of our solar system, and human explorers conduct research and

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:54.360
science just 300 miles above the surface of our planet, we pause to recognize and

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:59.580
honor a visionary astronaut for his courage and commitment to reaching our

00:20:59.580 --> 00:20:59.860
goal

